 # Rotating the ingest keys without a gap
 
-[TODO](TODO)
+A token is only worth anything if the key behind it can change. The day that key leaks, or the day
+an auditor asks when it was last replaced, the deployment has to be able to mint a new one, hand it
+to its clients, and put the old one away — while the clients that are mid-flight with a token signed
+by the old key keep working. That window is this page, and the whole of it is one arithmetic
+question: **when may the old key leave?**
+
+Everything here is about `lakelet/4.23-secret-rotation`. `lakelet/4.1-ingest-protocol` decided what a
+payload is, `lakelet/4.6-ingest-backpressure` decided what happens when the daemon cannot keep up,
+and `lakelet/4.10-downsampling` decided what this tool's answers look like: one `name=value` per
+line on stdout, one sentence on stderr, one exit code per class of failure. What none of them
+decided is who may connect at all.
 
 ## The ring is the switch
 
+The keys are files. One file per key, `<id>.key`, in the `keys` directory **below the store**
+(`store.dir`, the directory the daemon already owns):
+
+```text
+# this store's ring: one key per file, named <id>.key
+id=2026-10
+secret=9f2b0c…
+not_before_millis=1700000000000
+not_after_millis=1700006600000
+```
+
+`#` starts a comment, every other line is `name=value`, the file's name **is** the key's id, and a
+file that disagrees with its own name is refused by name. A secret is hex, in either case;
+`not_after_millis` is simply **absent** while the key is open, and it is written on the day the key
+is retired.
+
+That directory is the switch, and this is the part of the design a deployment has to know:
+
+- **no `keys` directory at all — authentication is off.** A deployment that has not turned
+  authentication on is a deployment with no keys, not a daemon that refuses everything: the first
+  payload of a connection is a batch, exactly as it was before this step, and a store that was
+  written by an older build keeps taking data;
+- **a `keys` directory that holds no `*.key` is a refusal.** An operator who created the ring and
+  then misspelled a file name has said "authenticate" and left nothing to authenticate with, and the
+  daemon refuses the connection with a sentence naming the directory rather than quietly letting
+  everybody in. Failing open is the one answer this must not give. Remove the directory to turn
+  authentication off.
+
+With a ring on disk, the **first payload of a connection is a token** and every payload after it is
+a batch. The token travels inside the same frame as everything else ([the protocol page](protocol.md)
+pins the layout: a length, a version byte, the payload, a checksum), so nothing about the wire
+format changed for this step — a token is a payload whose bytes are:
+
+```text
+ 0..2        u16, little endian: length of the client id in bytes
+ then        those bytes, UTF-8
+ then  u16, little endian: length of the key id in bytes
+ then        those bytes, UTF-8
+ then  u64, little endian: issued_millis
+ then  u64, little endian: expires_millis
+ then  64 bytes: the tag, as lower-case hex
+```
+
+and the tag is HMAC-SHA-256 under the key's secret over **the bytes before it** — the fields as they
+travel, length prefixes and all. The comparison of that tag goes through
+[`subtle`](https://docs.rs/subtle/2.6.1/subtle/trait.ConstantTimeEq.html)'s `ConstantTimeEq`, and
+the reason is one sentence: `==` on a tag stops at the first byte that is wrong, so the time it
+takes is a function of how much of a guess was right, and a tag is exactly the value an attacker
+gets to guess with as many requests as they like. This is the relay track's lesson
+(`relay/2.11-hmac-signing`) arriving in this project, which is where the dependency came from.
+
 ## The operator's procedure
 
+Three commands, and the order is the procedure:
+
+```sh
+# 1. a new key, with a window that opens now. The secret is yours: this build does not generate
+#    keys, because this course's randomness is the store's own seeded generator and a ring that came
+#    out of a seed would be the same ring on every machine. Use `openssl rand -hex 32`.
+lakeletq rotate --store ./data --add --id 2026-11 --secret "$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
+
+# 2. read the ring. `safe_retire` is the instant the key above is about: the last instant a token
+#    the old key signed can still be live.
+lakeletq rotate --store ./data --list
+
+# 3. retire the old key, at that instant or later. Retiring it earlier is refused, with the instant
+#    named — a key that leaves while a token it signed is live is a client locked out mid-flight.
+lakeletq rotate --store ./data --retire 2026-10
+
+# 4. and, a month later, the key that is now old again.
+lakeletq rotate --store ./data --list
+```
+
+Step 1 is the moment authentication turns on for a store that had none, and it is worth knowing
+that it takes effect **immediately**: the ring is read per connection, so a running daemon is
+authenticating its next client off the file that was just written. Nothing is restarted, and there
+is no window in which the daemon is serving under one key and the file says another.
+
+Step 3's default `--at` is the earliest instant a retirement is allowed at, so the safe thing is the
+easy thing. `--at <MILLIS>` names one explicitly, and anything under `safe_retire` is still refused:
+
+```sh
+$ lakeletq rotate --store ./data --retire 2026-10 --at 1700006000000
+lakeletq rotate: `2026-10` cannot be retired at 1700006000000: a token it signed could still be
+live until 1700006600000, which is the last instant the signing was taken over plus this
+deployment's longest token lifetime
+$ echo $?
+4
+```
+
+Nothing is written when that happens, and the exit code is `4` — the store refused — which is a
+class of failure this tool already had a number for. The rest of the classes are the ones
+`lakelet/4.10-downsampling` assigned: `2` a wrong call (no operation, two operations, a secret that
+is not hex, an instant that is not a number, a key name that would walk out of the ring's
+directory), `3` the thing asked about is not there (a store, or a key the ring does not hold), `1`
+this deployment is broken.
+
+### The ring, as this page was written
+
+The two keys below are the ones the commands above make, on a store of this page's own — `--add`
+with `--from 1700000000000`, then `--add --from 1700003000000`, then `lakeletq rotate --list`:
+
+```text
+id=old not_before=1700000000000 not_after=none preferred=false safe_retire=1700006600000
+id=new not_before=1700003000000 not_after=none preferred=true safe_retire=none
+```
+
+Three things in those two lines are the step. **Oldest first**, because that is the order a rotation
+happens in. **`preferred=true` is on exactly one key** — the one with the latest `not_before` whose
+window is still open, which is the key that signs new tokens; both keys verify while both are open.
+And **`safe_retire=none` means the ring cannot prove an instant for that key yet**, which is not a
+bug and not a missing number: nothing newer has taken the signing over from it, so it may still
+sign, so there is no instant after which its tokens are all gone. It is the number that tells an
+operator to add the successor before retiring anything.
+
 ## The arithmetic of the window
 
+A key may leave only after the last token it signed has expired. The ring holds no record of when a
+key arrived — only the `not_before` each file states — so the last instant a key *could* have signed
+is the latest `not_before` on the ring among the keys that are newer than it: the instant a
+successor existed that it could not outrank. A token signed at that instant is live until
+
+```text
+earliest_safe_retirement(key) = latest_not_before_of_a_newer_key + MAX_TOKEN_LIFETIME_MILLIS
+```
+
+and `MAX_TOKEN_LIFETIME_MILLIS` is **3600000** — one hour. It is the *longest* lifetime this build
+may issue, not the usual one, and that is the whole point: a bound built out of the lifetime most
+clients ask for is a bound that holds for every client except the one that asked for the longest
+token, which is precisely the client it strands. `issue` refuses a lifetime above that number for
+the same reason — a deployment that could issue a longer token than its own retirement arithmetic
+allows would have a bound that is not a bound.
+
+The bound is also the *latest* takeover rather than the earliest, and the difference is worth
+naming: with `a`, `b` and `c` on the ring, the files cannot say whether `b` or `c` took the signing
+over from `a`. The number this page prints is the later one, which means an operator waits at least
+as long as they strictly had to and never less — a retirement that is too late strands nobody, and a
+retirement that is too early is the failure this page exists for.
+
 ## What a gap costs
 
+Delete the old key while a client is holding a live token signed by it and that client is refused —
+not once, but for as long as the token would have lived. On the client's side it is a connection
+that closes at its first payload; on the deployment's side it is a **refusal** in the numbers it
+already reads: the connection's own `connection closed … rejected=N` line, the daemon's counters,
+and the `token refused` warning sitting above both with the reason on it. A refused key is
+`KeyRetired` and its sentence names the instant the key left; a token past its expiry is `Expired`
+and its sentence names both the instant it expired and the instant the connection arrived; a token
+whose tag does not verify is `Tag`; a token naming a key this deployment does not have is
+`UnknownKey`; a token claiming to be signed before its key's window opened is `NotYetValid`. A
+client whose *key* is gone has to be re-keyed rather than re-issued, which is why the retirement is
+the fact an operator sees first.
+
+What a gap does **not** cost is data: a refused connection never reaches the store's write path, so
+what the client is told is "you did not get in" and never "your frames were dropped".
+
 ## What this page does not do
 
+- **It does not generate keys.** The operator supplies the secret, and the page says so where the
+  command is: this build has one source of randomness and it is the store's own seeded generator
+  (`lakelet/4.13-model-based-tests`), which is the right thing for a walk that has to replay and the
+  wrong thing for key material. `openssl rand -hex 32` is one line away and is not this program's
+  business;
+- **there is no HSM and no key management service.** The secret is a file in the store directory, in
+  the same place as the data, with the same permissions and whatever backup policy the store has. A
+  deployment that needs the key to be unreadable by the process that uses it needs a different
+  program, and this page would rather say that than imply otherwise;
+- **there is no revocation list.** A retirement is a schedule — an instant a key stops being
+  verified at — and not a way to say "this token, right now, is bad". A key that has been *stolen*
+  is a retirement plus, at the limit, treating every token it signed as suspect; the ring has no
+  per-token state, and adding one would be a store of its own;
+- **nothing already written is re-encrypted or re-signed.** The ring is about who may open a
+  connection, and the records a client sent are the records the store keeps. Rotating the key does
+  not touch a byte of them;
+- **and the ring is read per connection rather than watched.** That is the cost this design chose:
+  one directory read and a handful of file reads per client, in exchange for a rotation with no
+  restart, no signal and no control channel. A deployment large enough for that read to matter would
+  watch the directory, or hand the ring out over the control channel it already has, and the ring's
+  reader is the same either way — but that is a change of transport, and this step did not build it.
+
 ## Where the code is
+
+- [`src/service/keys.rs`](../src/service/keys.rs) — the ring, the token, the typed refusals, and the
+  window arithmetic. It reads no clock: every function that depends on an instant takes one, which
+  is what makes "what does this ring do at this instant" a question a test answers with a number;
+- [`src/cli/rotate.rs`](../src/cli/rotate.rs) — the three operations, the refusal that keeps a
+  retirement from stranding a token, and the edit that changes one line of a key file and leaves the
+  rest of it alone;
+- [`src/service/ingest.rs`](../src/service/ingest.rs) — where the ring is read, where a connection's
+  instant is read (the daemon's only clock, once per connection), and where a refused client is
+  counted beside the frames that were refused for every other reason;
+- [`docs/dr-drill.md`](dr-drill.md) — the other page in this project that is an operator's
+  procedure, and the other one whose numbers are printed by a command the spec re-runs;
+- [`docs/cardinality.md`](cardinality.md) — the other refusal that is counted and reported rather
+  than logged and forgotten.
